{"answer":"We decide a fisher can’t return excess when the excess fish are too fragile to survive a return trip, the boat’s capacity is already full, or the fish have already been processed (cooked, salted, or packed) and can’t be put back into the lake without ruining the catch.  In that case the excess is treated as forfeited.  The forfeiture is entered in the shared ledger as a separate line item for that fisher with a note ‘forfeited excess’ and the amount is subtracted from that fisher’s allowance for the following month.  The community keeper keeps a tally of all forfeitures; if a fisher accumulates more than one forfeiture in a season the keeper will issue a temporary ban for the next round.  This keeps the rule fair and transparent for everyone.

Revised_policy: "Each fisher may take no more than 20% of the lake’s current fish mass on any single trip. Before each outing the fisher checks the current lake mass from the ledger. During the trip the fisher records the net weight taken (catch minus any returned excess) in the ledger. If the recorded weight exceeds 20% of the current lake mass, the excess must be returned to the lake or, if return is impossible (due to fragility, boat capacity, or processing), forfeited for the next month. All fish taken are for subsistence or household use, and the rule applies equally to all participants. The ledger keeps a running total of the lake’s mass, updated after each trip as described above. Community monitors compliance via the shared log; repeat violators are temporarily barred from fishing for one round. All records are entered in the shared phone ledger and kept in the community log for transparency." 

